<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Chadwick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir James Chadwick</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir James Chadwick was a British experimental physicist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, who studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a> at Manchester and later served as his assistant director of research at the Cavendish Laboratory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. Interned at Ruhleben in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, he went on to discover the neutron and measure its mass, for which he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1935. That year he moved to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, where he installed a cyclotron. In 1941 he wrote the final draft of the MAUD Report, and he headed the British team on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>; he was knighted in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-chadwick.jpg" alt="James Chadwick" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Los Alamos National Laboratory · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Chadwick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
